      "The canoe we would pick if we could only own one for rivers. It does nothing badly, handles moving water confidently, and forgives the mistakes that put beginners in the water.",
    description:
      "River paddling punishes specialization. A hull that carves beautifully in rapids is exhausting on the flat stretches between them, and a fast tripping hull is terrifying the moment the current starts pushing it sideways. The Mad River Explorer 16 is the canoe that has been resolving that tension for decades, and after a season on real rivers we understand why it keeps getting recommended. Its shallow-V hull gives it a defined keel line that tracks respectably on flatwater, while moderate rocker at both ends lets it turn far more willingly than a straight-keeled tripping canoe. What separates it from most all-rounders is secondary stability — the way it behaves when heeled over onto its side. Where a flat-bottomed canoe feels rock solid until it suddenly is not, the Explorer leans progressively and holds, which is exactly the behavior you want when a current grabs the hull or you need to edge the boat to carve around a rock. That characteristic is what makes it forgiving for paddlers still learning to read water. Capacity is generous enough for a tandem crew and a week of gear. The honest cost is weight: in polyethylene it is a genuine two-person carry, and it is not fast.",
  },
  {
    id: "esquif-prospecteur-15",
    name: "Esquif Prospecteur 15 (T-Formex)",
    category: "Tandem, 15 ft, T-Formex, Symmetrical Rocker",
    badge: "Best for Rocky Rivers",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.9,
    reviewCount: 310,
    pros: [
      "T-Formex hull slides over rock and rebounds from impacts that would crack composite",
      "Classic Prospector hull shape is among the best all-round river designs ever drawn",
      "Symmetrical rocker makes it genuinely maneuverable in technical water",
      "Lighter than polyethylene at comparable durability",
      "Holds resale value exceptionally well",
