          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why a River Canoe Is Different
          </h2>

          <p>
            On flat water, a canoe only has to deal with the forces you put into it. On a river, the water is doing things to the hull continuously and without asking — pushing one end faster than the other, grabbing at a submerged edge, accelerating around a rock. Every meaningful design difference in a river canoe exists to handle that.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first is <strong>maneuverability over tracking</strong>. A lake canoe is designed to go straight with minimal effort, because that is what covering distance on flat water requires. A river canoe has to change direction now, repeatedly, often in a boat length or less. That means more rocker, usually a shorter waterline, and a hull that will pivot rather than resist.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second, and the one beginners underestimate most, is <strong>secondary stability</strong>. Initial stability is how solid a canoe feels sitting flat — flat-bottomed hulls have loads of it. Secondary stability is how it behaves once heeled over onto its side. A flat-bottomed hull has a great deal of the first and very little of the second, which produces the specific failure mode that dumps beginners into rivers: the boat feels completely solid, then passes a tipping point and capsizes almost instantly. A shallow-arch or shallow-V hull feels less planted at rest and leans progressively, holding at an angle rather than letting go. In current, where you will be leaning the boat deliberately and where the water will lean it for you, that progressive behavior is worth far more than the reassuring feel of a flat bottom.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is <strong>hull material</strong>, which on a river is a safety consideration rather than a durability preference. This gets its own section below, because it is the decision most likely to be made badly.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
