        <div id="materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Hull Materials for Rocky Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            The material a canoe is built from determines how it behaves when it hits a rock, and on a river it will hit rocks. This is the single most consequential specification for river use, and it is worth understanding properly rather than sorting by price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">T-Formex</h3>
          <p>
            The successor to Royalex, and the material serious river paddlers ask for by name. It is a foam-cored sheet with tough outer skins that slides over rock, absorbs impact by deforming, and springs back to shape. It is lighter than polyethylene at comparable durability, which is why it commands a premium. If your river is rocky and your budget allows, this is the material to buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three-Layer Polyethylene</h3>
          <p>
            The workhorse. A foam core between two poly skins, producing a hull that is effectively indestructible for recreational river use and cheap to manufacture. The trade is weight — poly canoes are typically 10 to 20 pounds heavier than equivalent T-Formex — and a tendency to deform if stored badly in summer heat. For most river paddlers on a budget, this is the correct choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Composites (Fiberglass, Kevlar, Basalt)</h3>
          <p>
            Dramatically lighter, considerably more expensive, and less tolerant of impact. Standard fiberglass is a poor river material. Tougher layups built around basalt or aramid fibers — Nova Craft's TuffStuff being the example in this guide — are a legitimate river option for paddlers who value portage weight and are careful about what they run over. Composites are also field-repairable with a patch kit, which aluminum and poly are not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Aluminum</h3>
          <p>
            Nearly immortal and genuinely cheap used, but with one behavior that matters on rivers: aluminum grabs rock instead of sliding over it. A hull that stops abruptly against a submerged boulder in current is a hull that turns broadside and pins, which is how canoes wrap and how people get trapped. Aluminum is a fine choice for lakes and gentle rivers and a poor one for anything technical.
          </p>

