        <div id="rocker-and-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Rocker, Length, and Hull Shape
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three numbers describe most of how a canoe will behave on a river, and none of them appear prominently in marketing copy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rocker</h3>
          <p>
            Rocker is the upward curve of the hull from the middle toward each end, like the runners on a rocking chair. More rocker means less hull in the water at the bow and stern, so the canoe pivots easily — you can spin it into an eddy with one stroke. Less rocker means more hull engaged along the full length, so it holds a straight line and resists being turned. Whitewater canoes have a lot; tripping canoes have very little; river all-rounders sit in between. If you only remember one specification, remember this one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Length</h3>
          <p>
            Shorter canoes turn faster and are easier to handle alone. Longer canoes are faster over distance, carry more, and track better. On a tight, twisting river, 12 to 15 feet is genuinely more capable than 17 feet, and the extra length becomes a liability every time the river bends around a deadfall. On a wide river with long open stretches, that reverses completely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bottom Profile</h3>
          <p>
            Flat bottoms give strong initial stability and weak secondary stability, and they are the standard on cheap recreational canoes because they feel safe in a showroom. Shallow arch and shallow-V bottoms feel a little less planted at rest and behave far better once heeled, which is what current demands. A shallow V adds a defined keel line that helps a canoe track, which is why it appears on all-rounders that need to cover flatwater as well as rapids.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <div id="solo-vs-tandem" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Solo vs Tandem on Moving Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            The solo-versus-tandem question is usually framed as one of company, and on a river it is really one of control and logistics.
          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>solo canoe</strong> responds to one person's decisions immediately. There is no communication lag, no mismatch between what the bow paddler sees and what the stern paddler is doing, and no coordination required in the two seconds before you hit something. For a competent paddler in technical water, that immediacy is a genuine safety advantage. Solo boats are also far easier to load onto a car alone, which quietly determines how often a canoe gets used at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>tandem canoe</strong> is faster with two skilled paddlers, carries vastly more gear, and lets a stronger paddler take a less experienced one down water they could not run alone. The catch is that tandem paddling in current is a skill in itself. The bow paddler is the one who sees the rock and has the leverage to move the boat sideways; the stern paddler steers. When those two roles are not clearly understood, a tandem canoe in current becomes an argument that ends in the water. It is worth agreeing on commands before the trip rather than during it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our practical advice: if you mostly paddle alone or on small, tight rivers, buy solo first. If you are paddling with a partner on bigger water or doing multi-day trips with gear, buy tandem. Many river paddlers eventually own one of each, and a tandem canoe paddled solo from the bow seat, facing backward, is a serviceable stopgap.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6 -->
        <div id="outfitting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. River Outfitting You Actually Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            A canoe bought for a river is not ready for a river out of the box. Four things separate an outfitted boat from a bare one, and the first two are not optional.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">A Swamped Canoe Weighs Over a Thousand Pounds</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              A 16-foot canoe filled with water holds well over a hundred gallons, and water weighs about 8.3 lb per gallon. That mass, moving with the current, cannot be stopped by hand and will wrap around anything it meets with enough force to fold the hull. This is the reason flotation is not an accessory. Airbags fill the empty volume so a capsized canoe floats high, stays recoverable, and cannot fill completely.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Flotation bags</strong> go in the bow and stern, lashed to the thwarts, filling the empty volume where water would otherwise collect. For anything beyond gentle current they are essential, and they are cheap relative to what they prevent.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Painter lines</strong> — a length of floating rope secured at each end of the canoe — give you something to grab and something to control the boat with during a rescue. Fifteen to twenty feet at each end, secured so they cannot come loose and trail as an entanglement hazard, is standard.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Kneeling pads and thigh straps</strong> change how the boat handles more than most people expect. Kneeling drops your center of gravity and puts your body in contact with the hull, so you can lean and edge the canoe deliberately rather than just sitting on top of it. Thigh straps make that connection positive, letting you hold an edge through a turn. On technical water, they transform an all-round canoe.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A properly fitted PFD, worn</strong>, and a bailer or pump. Neither is interesting and both are the difference between an incident and an accident. On any river trip where the water is cold or the section is remote, add a throw bag and know how to use it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="matching-the-river" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Matching a Canoe to Your River
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Small, Twisting Creeks</h3>
          <p>
            Short and heavily rockered. A solo canoe in the 12 to 14 foot range, in polyethylene or T-Formex, will get down water that a 16-foot tandem simply cannot navigate without repeated broaching. Durability matters more here than anywhere, because shallow creeks mean constant rock contact.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mixed Rivers With Occasional Rapids</h3>
          <p>
            This describes most rivers, and it is where an all-rounder like the Explorer or the Aurora earns its keep. Moderate rocker, a shallow arch or shallow V, 15 to 16 feet. Efficient enough for the flat miles, agile enough for the interesting parts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Big Rivers and Long Trips</h3>
          <p>
            Length and efficiency win. A 16 to 17 foot tripping hull with minimal rocker covers distance with far less effort, and load capacity becomes a real constraint on multi-day trips. Accept that it will turn reluctantly and plan your lines earlier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Genuine Whitewater</h3>
          <p>
            Buy a whitewater canoe, in T-Formex, and outfit it fully with airbags, saddle, and thigh straps. An all-round hull can survive Class II; Class III demands a boat designed for it, and paddling skills to match. This is also the point where formal instruction stops being optional — moving-water rescue and swiftwater safety are learned skills, not intuitions.
          </p>
        </div>

