        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best canoe for river paddling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most river paddlers the Mad River Explorer 16 is the best all-round choice, combining moderate rocker for maneuverability with enough tracking for flat stretches and excellent secondary stability. For rocky, technical rivers the Esquif Prospecteur 15 in T-Formex is more durable and more agile. For long-distance river trips the Old Town Penobscot 164 is considerably more efficient over distance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What length canoe is best for rivers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For tight, twisting rivers, 12 to 15 feet turns far more easily and is the better choice. For wide rivers and long-distance trips, 16 to 17 feet tracks better, carries more gear, and is more efficient over distance. Sixteen feet is the most common compromise for tandem river paddling because it turns acceptably while still covering miles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best canoe material for rocky rivers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                T-Formex and three-layer polyethylene are the two best materials for rocky rivers because both slide over rock and rebound from impacts rather than cracking. T-Formex is lighter and more expensive; polyethylene is heavier and cheaper. Composite hulls are lighter still but damage more easily, and aluminum grabs rock rather than sliding over it, which can cause a canoe to pin in current.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is rocker on a canoe and why does it matter on rivers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rocker is the upward curve of the hull from the center toward the bow and stern, like the curve of a rocking chair. More rocker means less hull in the water at the ends, so the canoe pivots more easily — essential for turning quickly in rapids and around obstacles. Less rocker means the canoe tracks straighter and is more efficient on flatwater but turns reluctantly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need flotation bags in a canoe on a river?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For anything beyond gentle current, yes. Airbags fill the empty volume in the bow and stern so a swamped canoe floats high rather than filling completely with water. A canoe full of water weighs well over a thousand pounds and is effectively impossible to control or rescue, and it can pin against obstacles with enormous force. Airbags, painter lines at both ends, and a properly fitted PFD are the baseline river outfitting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I get a solo or tandem canoe for rivers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A solo canoe is easier to handle in tight water, easier to load and transport alone, and responds immediately to a single paddler's input. A tandem canoe carries far more gear, is faster with two competent paddlers, and is the right choice for trips. Many river paddlers eventually own both. If you paddle mostly alone on small rivers, buy solo first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes When Buying a River Canoe
          </h2>

          <p>
            The mistakes below account for most of the regret we hear from people who bought the wrong canoe, and every one of them is made in a shop rather than on the water.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a Flat-Bottomed Hull Because It Felt Stable in the Shop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flat bottoms feel wonderfully solid sitting still and behave badly once current leans them. The reassuring initial stability that sells canoes on a showroom floor is exactly the characteristic that produces sudden, unrecoverable capsizes in moving water. Judge a river canoe by how it feels heeled over, not by how it feels flat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Aluminum for a Rocky River</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cheap used aluminum canoe is a genuinely good buy for lakes and gentle rivers, and a poor one for rocky water. Aluminum catches on submerged rock rather than sliding over it, and a canoe that stops suddenly in current turns broadside and pins. Buy poly or T-Formex if your river has rocks in it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring How the Canoe Gets to the River</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 93-pound canoe that requires two people to load is a canoe that only gets used when two people are available, which in practice means rarely. Weight is not just a portage consideration; it determines how often the boat leaves the garage at all. Be honest about who is loading it and how.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Flotation and Painters to Save Money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Outfitting is the cheapest safety spending in paddling and the first thing people cut. Airbags, painter lines, a bailer, and a PFD that actually fits cost a small fraction of the canoe and are what make a capsize an inconvenience rather than an emergency. Budget for them as part of the boat, not as accessories to add later.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

